// What the harness has to be told about this application.
//
// Every surface names a `marker` -- a control that exists only there. Without
// one the harness answers "already here" for every surface, so a check's
// navigation step presses nothing and then fails on a control one press away.
//
// A marker has to be absent from the page being left, not merely present on
// the page being reached: "Sign up" is in the header of every page, so it
// proves nothing about having arrived at the signup form.
//
// It also has to have a *box*. Arrival is judged by finding a node whose name
// carries the marker and whose width and height are both above zero, and CI's
// host is built with no font catalogue: text there shapes to no glyphs, so a
// heading lays out at its line width and zero height. Both auth forms were
// named by their titles -- "Welcome back" and "Create an account" -- and both
// surfaces were therefore unreachable on that host. Not one check on either
// page ran; they reported "could not open: no visible, enabled, sized semantic
// control matching it", which reads as a missing link rather than as an
// unmeasurable marker. So each form is named by a field instead. A field is
// padded and bordered whether or not a glyph shapes, and "Username" and
// "Display name" each name exactly one node in this application.
AppProfile(
    surfaces: [
        (name: "home", opener: "Home", marker: Some("link:Get started")),
        // The dashboard is all text, so its marker is the card's `role="region"`
        // whose `aria-label` repeats its title. See `DashboardPage`: the region
        // exists so that this page has something with a box to be recognised by.
        (name: "dashboard", opener: "Dashboard", marker: Some("Next steps")),
        (name: "login", opener: "Log in", marker: Some("Username")),
        (name: "signup", opener: "Sign up", marker: Some("Display name")),
    ],
    navigation_controls: ["Home", "Dashboard", "Log in", "Sign up"],
    home_opener: Some("Home"),
    // The template's attribution, which points at the product this starter
    // authenticates against. Following it would make a run depend on
    // honey.id being up rather than on this application.
    manual_controls: [
        (label: "Secure Auth by Honey", command: "honey.id"),
    ],
)
